e opportunity  to envision your character in in his the  body that they might give him it they  continued to evolve but you had a notion  of what that was and you knew what he  was going to what his music was going to  sound like and what his words were going  to sound like and so you had a wonderful  place to start and as everyone has  indicated Howard had a way of  communicating these characters to you on  a level that he could play all of them  he did but but it was thrilling you fell  in love with your character immediately  and Gaston is a lot to get to fall in  love with I mean it really is I mean  come on  so  what could they mean so I got the  opportunity to sort of revel in that and  it doesn't really get any better than  that  so andreas it's I think any animator  will tell you that the hardest thing to  do or to animate is a human form  especially one that's you know you want  to be believable so in a way I think you  were given a really tough job and at the  same time you were also given the job of  you know doing this kind of there as he  said good-looking villain so there's  some extra work happening that yeah  extra work happening there so could you  talk about the challenges of I think EXO  actually guest on started out as an  entirely different design if I'm not  mistaken so can you talk about yeah a  little bit of a hard time getting a hold  of Gaston visually I mean I understood  the character from a story point of view  and Rich's readings but but to set his  look was really hard because I took a  look at what the story people had done  and they drew him as a cartoon character  you know sort of caricature with a big  jar I kept it hook and a mustache and I  thought I'm gonna have some fun with  this guy so I did my first scene which  was doing the opening song with that  model in mind big jaw big muscles and  all that and Jeffrey Katzenberg or then  boss said well he said the acting is  fine but he's not handsome enough I'm  going handsome he's t